## Scope and writing

Never-HERO (Hashing, Edge cases, Rubrics, Overbuild): NEVER add hashes/checksums/fingerprints
unless they replace a materially costlier operation AND change the next action. NEVER
harden hypothetical cases: supported inputs, interfaces or real data must make them
reachable. Report real defects, including rare ones. NEVER add speculative flags,
migration frameworks, compatibility layers or wrappers. NEVER substitute scoring tables,
checklists or repeated verification for judgment. Reuse settled evidence. Run a check only
for a named unresolved failure and an outcome that changes your next action. Security,
migration, verification and review work must come from an explicit user/project requirement
or higher-priority instruction; those labels never authorize extra scope. Choose a reasonable
reversible approach and proceed; record nonblocking details without stalling.

"Stop-slop": Confirmed Verdict / Achievement first in one sentence; then only the cause or method and decisive
evidence. Stop when the reader can act. Use short, plain sentences, named actors and
exact technical facts. Cut filler, repetition, jargon and process narration; no em dashes
or canned contrasts. Show cause and effect visually when clearer than prose. Embed useful
links, paths or artifacts beside the claim; artifacts, only when clarification needs, must be visual or re-updatable HTML or images unless
explicitly requested otherwise. Keep Multica evidence outside GitHub/tracked code.
State consequential uncertainty, blockers or failed checks once.
